SELECT '87c9cc49-fefd-51fd-b5d0-4b31a60c33b6','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='87c9cc49-fefd-51fd-b5d0-4b31a60c33b6' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '8c732d73-6323-5f72-8a6c-183f1a4aa82d','Band coupling','In multiband dynamics processing, the linking of two or more frequency bands so their detectors or gain changes act together rather than fully separately, for example forcing adjacent bands to share a common gain-reduction amount. Coupling reduces the spectral shifts and unnatural tonal changes that can occur when bands move completely independently.','A multiband compressor splits sound into frequency ranges called bands. Band coupling ties some bands together so they move as a group. This keeps the overall tone steadier. It stops one band from standing out oddly.','It exists to preserve tonal balance by preventing one band from being turned down far more than its neighbours, which would audibly recolour the sound. Coupling trades some independence for a more natural result.','When multiband compression on a full mix causes the tone to shift as bands react separately, an engineer increases band coupling or linking to keep the spectral balance stable.','Multiband dynamics',ARRAY['Multiband compression','Band independence','Crossover','Gain reduction','Detector','Spectral balance']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing band coupling, which links band gains, with the crossover that sets each band''s frequency edges.','Leaving all bands fully coupled, which reduces the multiband tool to something close to a single-band compressor.','Assuming more bands always means better results, ignoring how their interaction changes the tone.']::text[],ARRAY['Stabilising the tone of a mastered mix so multiband action does not audibly shift its balance.','Linking low bands on a bass so separate movement does not wobble the low end.','Reducing tonal artefacts when several bands react to the same loud event.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='8c732d73-6323-5f72-8a6c-183f1a4aa82d' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Band coupling'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '8c732d73-6323-5f72-8a6c-183f1a4aa82d','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='8c732d73-6323-5f72-8a6c-183f1a4aa82d' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '6bb4e5d4-30b7-560a-8937-bb9c99500c6e','Band independence','In multiband dynamics processing, the property whereby each frequency band is detected and processed on its own, with its own threshold, ratio, and time constants, so gain changes in one band do not directly affect the others. High band independence lets a problem in one region be treated without altering the rest of the spectrum.','A multiband compressor splits sound into frequency ranges called bands. Band independence means each band works on its own. You can squeeze one range and leave the others alone. This lets you fix one problem without touching the rest.','It exists to allow frequency-specific control, so an engineer can tame a harsh or loud region while leaving well-balanced regions untouched. This targeted control is the main reason to choose a multiband tool.','An engineer relies on band independence to compress only a boomy low-mid region or a harsh presence region of a mix, without squashing the whole signal.','Multiband dynamics',ARRAY['Multiband compression','Band coupling','Crossover','Threshold','Dynamic equalization','Frequency band']::text[],ARRAY['Over-processing a single band until its tone no longer matches the untouched neighbouring bands.','Confusing independent multiband control with equalization, which changes level statically rather than by dynamics.','Setting each band separately without listening to how they sum back together across the crossovers.']::text[],ARRAY['Controlling only a resonant low-mid build-up on a full mix while leaving the highs alone.','Taming sibilance-region energy on a vocal without dulling its body.','De-emphasising a loud kick band on a drum bus without affecting the cymbals.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='6bb4e5d4-30b7-560a-8937-bb9c99500c6e' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Band independence'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
